id: pm-gxs55a
title: "The ordering graph is flat: 1763 of 1991 sequenced nodes have no predecessor and the critical path is 6, so every scheduling analytic describes a workspace that barely has an order"
description: "Measured 2026-07-27. pm graph analyze reports node_count 2121 and edge_count 9262 over a fully connected corpus, component_count 1 and orphan_count 0, which is a genuine achievement of the terminal backfill. The ordering layer underneath it is a different story. Of those 9262 edges only 601 express sequence: blocked_by 383 and blocks 218, or 6.5 percent. Everything else is hierarchy or association: parent 1943, child 15, implements 1973, related 4437, verifies 129, discovered_from 161, incident_from 2, supersedes 1. The consequence is reported directly by the analyzer: layer_count 7, critical_path_length 6, and frontier_count 1763 out of ordered_node_count 1991, so 88.5 percent of sequenced nodes have no predecessor at all. Every analytic that consumes the ordering DAG therefore describes a nearly flat structure regardless of how much history the workspace holds. The critical path names six items out of two thousand. Slack, dominators and layering are computed over a shape that is one hub and a wide flat rim, with pm-mpbb at degree 209. Readiness in pm next is trivially satisfiable because almost nothing has an unmet predecessor. This is not the same gap as the reachability invariant, which asks whether every item reaches an outcome milestone within a bounded number of hops and therefore constrains paths from above. This asks the opposite question and finds the structure degenerate from below: the corpus records what work belongs to what, and almost never what had to happen before what. Because the ordering edges are the ones that carry time, the project timeline is the part of the graph that traversal cannot recover."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[8]: "area:graph","area:sdk",context-management,depth,governance,relationships,scale,traceability
created_at: "2026-07-27T04:07:13.692Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-01T10:05:27.473Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 480
acceptance_criteria: "Ordering-graph shape is a reported profile in its own right: predecessor coverage, layer distribution, and longest-path length are measured and trended, not implied by a critical path sample; A stated policy distinguishes items that legitimately have no predecessor from items whose predecessor was simply never recorded, so the frontier count becomes interpretable; Sequencing edges recovered for terminal work come from durable evidence such as history instants, release boundaries, shared artefacts and explicit references, and ambiguity is retained rather than resolved by fabrication; Scheduling analytics declare the fraction of the corpus they actually order, so a critical path over 6 of 2121 nodes is never presented as a workspace-wide result; The profile stays inside the graph analytics token and latency budgets and remains bounded at million-item scale"
goal: project management = context management
objective: "Make the project timeline recoverable by traversal, so graph algorithms can reconstruct what preceded what and not only what belongs to what"
value: A connected graph that cannot order itself answers structural questions but not historical ones; ordering edges are what turn the tracker into a timeline
impact: "Critical path, slack, dominators and readiness all currently describe a 6-deep DAG and will keep doing so at any corpus size"
why_now: "The terminal backfill closed the connectivity gap and made this the next visible one: 100 percent of nodes are reachable and 88.5 percent of them have no predecessor"
parent: pm-96h7
risk: medium
confidence: high
severity: medium
environment: "pm 2026.7.27, this workspace at 2118 items, measured via pm graph analyze --json and pm graph audit --summary"
repro_steps: Run pm graph analyze --json and read execution.frontier_count against execution.ordered_node_count and execution.critical_path_length; cross-check edge composition with pm graph audit --summary and read profile.edges_by_kind
expected_result: Longest-path depth and predecessor coverage grow with the recorded history of the project
actual_result: "critical_path_length 6, layer_count 7, frontier_count 1763 of 1991, and 6.5 percent of edges carry sequence"
