comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-29T17:04:16.703Z","harness:claude-code","Ordering graph re-measured 2026-07-29 at build 2026.7.29. Unchanged in every respect that matters, and this is the expected result.\n\nOrdered node count 2,069, layer count 7, frontier count 1,815, critical path length 6, cycle count 8. The reported critical path is still the same 2026-03-07 duplicate cluster - pm-phpq, pm-204c, pm-iziy, pm-f0e9, pm-mn6w, pm-wjdr, pm-a5ea - wired by the terminal backfill.\n\nThree ordering edges were added this session and all three are blocked_by rows on the dependent item, recording a real prerequisite discovered by measurement rather than inferred from co-occurrence: the graph conformance matrix is blocked on shape-capable corpus generation because its own fixture criterion names shapes no generator can produce; the episode substrate is blocked on the workspace-restore durability defect because episode resets run through restore; the retrieval quality contract is blocked on the gate actually being wired. No ordering edge was manufactured from release cohorts, parent lineage or chronology, which the extraction scan already established as a refusal class at a 67 percent drop rate.\n\nThe frontier ratio is therefore still 87.7 percent and the conclusion this item carries stands: the flat ordering graph is an authoring gap, not an extraction gap, and the lever is capture at write time. Every prerequisite recorded this session was written at the moment it was discovered, which is exactly the behaviour the lever needs to make routine.\n"
  "2026-07-31T06:14:46.233Z","harness:claude-code","Refreshed 2026-07-31 after this pass's enrichment: 1,839 of 2,096 ordered nodes have no predecessor (87.7 percent), critical path 9, makespan 10, eight known legacy ordering cycles, 464 of 2,226 nodes scheduled at all. Fifteen blocked_by edges were added this pass on active items with recorded evidence, which moved the frontier fraction by less than a tenth of a point: the flatness is dominated by the 1,904 terminal items, and authoring ordering among those is blocked_by pm-wrbe until closed_at exists, because every such write re-buckets the changelog."
  "2026-07-31T10:17:22.390Z","harness:codex","Ordering profile after the 2026-07-31 ecosystem organization: 2,100 ordered nodes, 1,841 frontier nodes, 10 layers, critical path length 9, and eight terminal-only legacy cycles. Seventeen evidence-backed blocked_by edges were added to active outcome paths; frontier fell by one, confirming the documented diagnosis that active authoring discipline helps but cannot repair terminal chronology at portfolio scale. Historical enrichment remains gated on deterministic closed_at and changelog-safe waves, not inference from mutable updated_at."
  "2026-07-31T14:15:54.513Z","harness:claude-code","Mechanical cause identified 2026-07-31, filed as pm-xm0id4. This item's measurement is confirmed and its attribution needs revising.\n\nThe measurement holds and has moved slightly: pm graph analyze today reports node_count 2234, edge_count 10167, layer_count 10, critical_path_length 9, and frontier_count 1845 of ordered_node_count 2104, so 87.7 percent of sequenced nodes still have no predecessor. This item's description attributes that to a corpus that records what work belongs to what and almost never what had to happen before what - that is, to under-authoring.\n\nThe corpus says otherwise. clearClosedBlockerSignals in src/cli/commands/close.ts:626 removes every dependency row whose kind is blocked_by when an item is closed, unconditionally and regardless of whether the blocker is still open. Counted over every tracked item file: of 1,900 closed items exactly ONE retains a blocked_by row, while 91 of 318 open items hold one. The reverse spelling blocks is untouched by that code path and shows the mirror-image distribution: 189 closed items hold a blocks row against 4 open ones.\n\nThat 1-versus-189 split across the same population is not an authoring pattern. Ordering was authored on the blocked endpoint at least as often as on the blocker endpoint, and the close path deleted it every time. Reproduced in an isolated sandbox: an item holding three blocked_by rows to three still-open blockers, plus one related row, retains only the related row after close, and the close history entry lists dependencies among its changed_fields while pm get --at reconstructs the deleted edges.\n\nTwo consequences for this item's acceptance criteria. The criterion asking for a stated policy distinguishing items that legitimately have no predecessor from items whose predecessor was never recorded needs a third category: items whose predecessor WAS recorded and was deleted at close. And the criterion asking for recovered sequencing edges from durable evidence cannot be satisfied by writing blocked_by rows onto terminal work, because those rows do not survive the next close - which is the specification pm-ye46ns currently carries.\n\nThe predecessor-coverage baseline to trend against is therefore not the frontier count alone but the survival split: blocked_by on 1 of 1,900 closed items versus blocks on 189."
  "2026-08-01T10:05:27.473Z","harness:claude-code","Ordering deficit re-measured 2026-08-01 after pm-xm0id4 closed. Whole-graph execution view: 2,124 ordered nodes, 10 layers, critical path 9, frontier 1,853 -- so 87.2 percent of ordered nodes still have no predecessor. Structural counts over the 1,913 closed items: 8 retain a blocked_by row, 186 carry the reverse blocks spelling, 1,913 carry at least one dependency of some kind. Two conclusions for planning. The flatness is not a connectivity problem -- every terminal item is in the graph -- it is specifically a predecessor problem, and it is now split cleanly in two: prospectively fixed by pm-xm0id4's lifecycle preservation, and historically recoverable by pm-ye46ns, whose only remaining blocker is pm-wrbe. The 186 reverse-spelling rows are the cheapest reconciliation target because the same real prerequisite recorded from the other endpoint should appear in both populations, which is the test the state-erased-signal-recovery catalog entry on pm-4k6b names as the one worth running first."
notes[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-27T04:19:18.690Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence 2026-07-27: the reported critical path is not a work sequence, it is a duplicate cluster.\n\npm graph analyze returns execution.critical_path as pm-phpq, pm-204c, pm-iziy, pm-f0e9, pm-mn6w, pm-wjdr, pm-a5ea. Reading those seven items shows what the chain actually is. They are titled Release-readiness maintenance loop 2026-03-07 run 2, run 3, and so on: a set of near-identical items produced by a repeating maintenance loop on a single day in March 2026. Their own history records the relationship explicitly. Each of the trailing six carries a comment written on 2026-04-04 by a dedupe pass reading, verbatim, that this item is superseded by pm-phpq for cluster release-loop::2026-03-07.\n\nSo the longest path the analyzer can find through this workspace is six hops of duplicate consolidation. The edges that carry it are related edges, all created at one instant on 2026-07-26 by the terminal-history backfill, which is exactly the right thing for that backfill to have recorded and exactly the wrong thing to present as a critical path. The analytic is not wrong about the graph; the graph does not contain a longer chain of anything else.\n\nTwo consequences worth separating.\n\nThe first is that the depth deficit this item describes is worse than the raw number suggests. A critical path of six sounds shallow; a critical path of six that turns out to be one item and five copies of it means the true longest sequence of causally ordered work in a two-thousand-item corpus spanning months is shorter than six. There is no ordered spine at all, only a wide flat rim and one hub.\n\nThe second is a concrete remediation that costs nothing to justify, because the evidence is machine-written and unambiguous. Those duplicate-consolidation relationships are supersedes, not related. Retyping them removes fabricated ordering signal from the execution DAG at the same time as it makes the consolidation lineage traversable, so it improves both halves of the problem with one change and no judgement call. A first tranche of this was applied on 2026-07-27 under the evidence rules in pm-lnrk: 39 supersedes edges recovered from durable text, taking the kind from 1 edge to 40 across the corpus. The remaining work is not more extraction, it is deciding whether the analyzer should treat related as an ordering edge at all, which is the question this item and pm-4020c5 meet on."
  "2026-07-27T12:53:40.774Z","harness:claude-code","Correction and new evidence, 2026-07-27 (second look at the reported critical path).\n\nThe existing note on this item states that the edges carrying the reported critical path were \"all created at one instant on 2026-07-26 by the terminal-history backfill\". The item files contradict that. Reading the dependency rows directly:\n\n  pm-204c -> pm-phpq  related  2026-03-07T07:48:09Z  author maintainer-agent\n  pm-iziy -> pm-204c  related  2026-03-07T08:03:43Z  author maintainer-agent\n  pm-f0e9 -> pm-iziy  related  2026-03-07T08:20:06Z  author maintainer-agent\n  pm-mn6w -> pm-f0e9  related  2026-03-07T09:31:15Z  author maintainer-agent\n  pm-wjdr -> pm-mn6w  related  2026-03-07T09:48:11Z  author maintainer-agent\n  pm-a5ea -> pm-wjdr  related  2026-03-07T10:12:40Z  author maintainer-agent\n\nSix of the seven chain edges were written live on 2026-03-07, each run linking to the run before it, minutes apart, by the agent that was executing the loop. Only the hub edges (every run also pointing at pm-phpq) came from the 2026-07-26 backfill, at 17:00-17:28 that day.\n\nThis changes the conclusion. The longest path in this workspace is not an artefact of a backfill and it is not six copies of one item. It is a real sequential record: runs 2 through 8 of a release-readiness maintenance loop, executed in that order on one morning, with each step recording its predecessor at the moment it happened. It is the only genuinely ordered chain the corpus contains, and it was recorded by an agent that simply wrote down what came before. The depth deficit is therefore not that the project never had order; it is that this discipline was applied on one day in March and essentially never again.\n\nThe second finding is an ambiguity that must be retained rather than resolved, and it is exactly the case this item's third acceptance criterion anticipates. Two durable records disagree about the direction of the same relationship. The 2026-03-07 edges say run N+1 follows run N, so the sequence runs phpq, 204c, iziy, f0e9, mn6w, wjdr, a5ea. The machine-written 2026-04-04 dedupe comments say each of the trailing runs is superseded by pm-phpq, and supersedes read as an ordering kind places the superseded item before its replacement, which reverses every one of those pairs. Two supersedes edges from that reading are already applied (phpq over 204c and over a5ea). Retyping the six chain edges to blocked_by while those supersedes edges exist would close a cycle through pm-phpq. Both records are true about different things: the runs really did happen in that order, and the consolidation really did pick phpq as the survivor. The graph cannot express both while supersedes and blocked_by are read into the same execution DAG.\n\nThat is a design question for this item and pm-4020c5 to settle, not a data repair: either supersedes stops contributing to the execution DAG, or consolidation lineage needs a kind that is explicitly not an ordering claim. No edges were changed on this cluster pending that decision."
  "2026-07-31T19:47:23.293Z","harness:claude-code","Measurement refresh 2026-07-31 (evening), at HEAD a66797e37. This item's headline numbers are stale in a way that changes what remains to be done, so recording the current figures rather than re-arguing the finding.\n\n                        this item as filed    2026-07-31\n  ordered_node_count            1991            2115\n  frontier_count (no pred)      1763            1853\n  critical_path_length             6               9\n  layer_count                      -              10\n\nThe frontier ratio barely moved: 88.5 percent then, 87.6 percent now. The critical path grew by half.\n\nThat split is the useful part, and it is diagnostic rather than cosmetic. The ordering work done since this item was filed - the outcome-milestone ladder being sequenced against itself on 07-29 and 07-31, plus scattered per-item edges like the one added today - deepened the SPINE without touching the FRONTIER. So the depth complaint in this item's title is now substantially answered while the flatness complaint is not: 1,853 of 2,115 sequenced nodes still have no predecessor, which is 90 more absolute than when the item was filed.\n\nThe deepest chain now reads end to end, and it is worth quoting because it is the argument for why the spine work mattered:\n\n  pm-1jrdri -> pm-pbyu -> pm-0z7n -> pm-6apl -> pm-1kxs -> pm-j82fd3 -> pm-9rgaal -> pm-o87av6 -> pm-xp1xsw -> pm-a5w7vv\n\nAn uncontracted noun defect, to the grammar ADR, to the alias substrate, to the ops consolidation, to the one-screen agent story, to the grammar milestone, to SDK-complete, to proven scale, to the GA gate. A single contract defect is now provably nine hops upstream of general availability, which is the kind of statement this graph exists to support and could not make at critical path 6.\n\nConsequence for this item's scope, proposed not applied: the remaining work is frontier reduction, not depth. Those are different jobs with different acceptance. Depth is improved by sequencing containers against each other, which is cheap, done, and nearly exhausted. Frontier reduction means authoring a predecessor for individual leaf items, which is the expensive authoring gap - and per the standing finding that pm close deletes blocked_by rows, it cannot be done durably on closed items at all until that deletion is addressed. Splitting the acceptance criteria along that line would let the depth half close on evidence while the frontier half stays open honestly.\n\nOne caveat on the acyclic field: analyze now reports acyclic false with cycle_count 8, whereas the lifecycle validate check reports ok with dependency_cycle_count 0 and no sample paths. The two count over different edge-kind sets - lifecycle uses ordering kinds only since PR#554, analyze spans all kinds including deliberately reciprocal related pairs. Not a regression and not introduced by today's edges; noted so a later pass does not read analyze's cycle_count as a lifecycle defect."
body: ""
